<http://data.silknow.org/object/e6b7f279-091c-3680-aa4c-c54c597d7e30>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Metallic lace border, built up from chains of thread made with a hooked needle. The very open pattern consists of a curving open stem decorated with simplified leaves and forming compartments containing stylised flowers. It is reminiscent both of the later Baroque laces and of the small-patterned Brussels lace of ca. 1700. \n\nTechnically the lace is a form of crochet and may be the chain lace mentioned in English inventories and accounts of the 17<sup>th</sup> century.\n\nThe thread is of copper-gilt wound on a yellow silk core."@en , "Metallic lace border, England, ca.1700"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/341fdd6d-ad85-366f-b5fe-37e5ad8d816d> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Metallic lace border, built up from chains of thread made with a hooked needle. The very open pattern consists of a curving open stem decorated with simplified leaves and forming compartments containing stylised flowers. It is reminiscent both of the later Baroque laces and of the small-patterned Brussels lace of ca. 1700. \n\nTechnically the lace is a form of crochet and may be the chain lace mentioned in English inventories and accounts of the 17<sup>th</sup> century.\n\nThe thread is of copper-gilt wound on a yellow silk core."@en , "Metallic lace border, England, ca.1700"@en ;
